r's work where  they collected different brain tumor  samples from different children and  recognize that they have different Amino  phenotypes and so the goal of the  original study was to try to treat those  different brain cancers by targeting the  amino phenotypes and the the data was  aggregated and a random forest model was  applied to it that found a 91 accuracy  and that came from a different source so  the goal here is to reproduce those  results that were originally found in  that  and so I did was I first explored the  data using PCA and heat maps to try to  get an overall big picture of what the  data looked like and with the PCA I had  found that there wasn't any clear  distinguishable groupings with the  different brain samples or within the  different non-tumor normal samples so it  didn't look very promising initially so  I used lazy predict to try to run  a large number of  classification models all at once and  get an estimate of which would be able  to work with the data most successfully  and lazy predict actually predicted  random force to be one of the top five  models so I ended up proceeding with  that classification method and from  there I looked at which features were  deemed significant in the random Forest  classification  and here's an example of the confusion  Matrix that came from using the random  Forest data and we ultimately got a 97.4  percent accuracy we can see the only  misclassified  cancer subtype there's the one pilotic  astrocytoma  however that also had the second fewest  number of test samples after using a  test transplant of 30 of the test data  and so it's not too surprising that the  results are off but it's very  beneficial to see that the results were  so successful throughout for each brain  type brain cancer type and for the  normal non-tuber examples  and so the other part of the project  that I did was I took I looked at all  the combined data so the five different  cancer subtypes versus normal I grouped  all t